\begin_layout Title
Chapter 13: Maximum Likelihood Methods
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
13.1 Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

MLE is generally the most efficient estimation procedure in the class of
 estimators that use information on the distribution of the endogenous variables
 given the exogenous variables.
 However, efficiency usually comes at the price of nonrobustness, and this
 is certainly the case for maximum likelihood.
 Maximum likelihood estimators are generally inconsistent if some part of
 the specified distribution is misspecified.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
13.2 Preliminaries and Examples
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Traditional maximum likelihood theory for independent, identically distributed
 observations 
\begin_inset Formula $\{y_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{G}:i=1,2,\ldots\}$
\end_inset

 starts by specifying a family of densities for 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 In almost all economic applications, we are interested in estimating parameters
 of 
\emph on
conditional
\emph default
 distributions.
 Therefore, we assume that each random draw is partitioned as 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},y_{i})$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{K}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{G}$
\end_inset

, and we are interested in estimating a model for the conditional distribution
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 We are not interested in the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, so we will not specify a model for it.
 Consequently, the method we will focus on is properly called 
\series bold
conditional maximum likelihood estimation (CMLE)
\series default
.
 By taking 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 to be null we cover unconditional MLE as a special case.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It is important to understand that the subsequent results apply any time
 we have random sampling in the cross section dimension.
 Thus, the general theory applies to system estimation, including panel
 data, provided we are willing to assume a distribution for 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In order to perform maximum likelihood analysis, we need to specify, or
 derive from an underlying (structural) model, the density of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 We assume this density is known up to a finite number of unknown parameters,
 with the result that we have a 
\series bold
parametric model
\series default
 of a conditional density.
 The vector (or scalar) 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 can be continuous or discrete, or it can have both discrete and continuous
 characteristics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*

\emph on
(Binary Response Model, specifically the Probit Model)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose that the 
\series bold
latent variable
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

 follows
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}^{*}=x_{i}^{\prime}\theta+e_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\emph on
independent
\emph default
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 (which is a 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

 vector with first element equal to unity for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

), 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of parameters, and 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}\sim N(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
 Instead of observing 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

, we observe only a binary variable indicating the sign of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}=\begin{cases}
1 & \mbox{if}\ y_{i}^{*}>0\\
0 & \mbox{if}\ y_{i}^{*}\leq0
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can easily obtain the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
P(y_{i}=1|x_{i}) & = & P(y_{i}^{*}>0|x_{i})\nonumber \\
 & = & P(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta+e_{i}>0|x_{i})\nonumber \\
 & = & P(e_{i}>-x_{i}^{\prime}\theta|x_{i})=1-\Phi(-x_{i}^{\prime}\theta)=\Phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta)
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 denotes the standard normal cumulative distribution function (cdf).
 Therefore,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(y_{i}=0|x_{i})=1-\Phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We can combine equations (3) and (4) into the density of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
f(y_{i}|x_{i})=\left[\Phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta)\right]^{y_{i}}\left[1-\Phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta)\right]^{1-y_{i}},\ y_{i}=0,1
\end{equation}

\end_inset

That 
\begin_inset Formula $f(y_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 is zero when 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}\notin\{0,1\}$
\end_inset

 is obvious.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
13.3 General Framework for Conditional Maximum Likelihood Estimation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{o}(y|x)$
\end_inset

 denote the conditional density of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}=x$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 are dummy arguments.
 We index this density by 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

o
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 to emphasize that it is the true density of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, and not just one of many candidates.
 It useful to let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathscr{X}\subset\mathbb{R}^{K}$
\end_inset

 denote the possible values for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathscr{Y}$
\end_inset

 denote the possible values of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

; 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathscr{X}$
\end_inset

and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathscr{Y}$
\end_inset

 are called the 
\emph on
supports
\emph default
 of the random vectos 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

, respectively.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For a general treatment, we assume that, for all 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathscr{X}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{o}(\cdot|x)$
\end_inset

 is a density with respect to a 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset

-
\emph on
finite measure
\emph default
, denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $v(dy)$
\end_inset

, which can be chosen to allow 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 to be discrete, continuous, or some mixture of the two.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 is discrete, the measure 
\begin_inset Formula $v(dy)$
\end_inset

 simply turns all integrals into sums; when 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 is purely continuous, we obtain the usual Riemann integrals.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The result from proability that is crucial for applying the analogy principle
 is the 
\series bold
conditional Kullback-Leibler information inequality
\series default
.
 The following statement suffices for our purpose: for any nonegative function
 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\cdot|x)$
\end_inset

 such that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \int_{\mathscr{Y}}}f(y|x)v(dy)=1,\ \forall\ x\in\mathscr{X}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Property CD.1 in chapter 13's appendix implies that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathscr{K}(f;x)\equiv{\displaystyle \int_{\mathscr{Y}}}\log\left[\nicefrac{p_{o}(y|x)}{f(y|x)}\right]p_{o}(y|x)v(dy)\geq0,\ \forall\ x\in\mathscr{X}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Because the integral is identically zero for 
\begin_inset Formula $f=p_{o}$
\end_inset

, expression (7) says that, for each 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathscr{K}(f;x)$
\end_inset

 is minimized at 
\begin_inset Formula $f=p_{o}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can apply inequality (7) to a parametric model for 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{o}(\cdot|x)$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left\{ f(\cdot|x;\theta),\ \theta\in\Theta,\ \Theta\subset\mathbb{R}^{P}\right\} 
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which we assume satisfies condition (6) for each 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathscr{X}$
\end_inset

 and each 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\in\Theta$
\end_inset

; if it does not, then 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\cdot|x;\theta)$
\end_inset

 does not integrate to unity (with respect to the measure 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

), and as as a result, it is a very poor candidate for 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{o}(y|x)$
\end_inset

.
 Model (8) is a 
\series bold
correctly specified model of the conditional density
\series default
, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{o}(\cdot|\cdot)$
\end_inset

, if, for some 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}\in\Theta$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
f(\cdot|x;\theta_{o})=p_{o}(\cdot|x),\ \forall\ x\in\mathscr{X}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

As before, we use 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 to distinguish the true value of the parameter from a generic element of
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For each 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathscr{X}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathscr{K}(f,x)$
\end_inset

 can be written as 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left\{ \log\left[p_{o}(y_{i}|x_{i})\right]|x_{i}=x\right\} -E\left\{ \log\left[f(y_{i}|x_{i})\right]|x_{i}=x\right\} $
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, if the parametric model is correctly specified, then 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left\{ \log\left[f(y_{i}|x_{i};\theta_{o})\right]|x_{i}\right\} \geq E\left\{ \log\left[f(y_{i}|x_{i};\theta)\right]|x_{i}\right\} $
\end_inset

, or
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E\left[l_{i}(\theta_{o})|x_{i}\right]\geq E\left[l(\theta)|x_{i}\right],\ \theta\in\Theta
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
l_{i}(\theta)\equiv l_{i}\left(y_{i},x_{i},\theta\right)\equiv\log\ f(y_{i}|x_{i};\theta)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

is the 
\series bold
conditional log likelihood for observation i
\series default
.
 Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $l_{i}(\theta)$
\end_inset

 is a 
\emph on
random
\emph default
 funciton of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

, since it depends on the random vector 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},y_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 By taking the expected value of expression (10) and using iterated expectations
, we see that 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 solves
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\max_{\theta\in\Theta}\ E\left[l_{i}(\theta)\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the expectation is with respect to the joint distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},y_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 The sample analogue of expression (12) is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \max_{\theta\in\Theta}}\ N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\log f(y_{i}|x_{i};\theta)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

A solution to problem (13), assuming that one exists, is the 
\series bold
conditional maximum likelihood estimator
\series default
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

, which we denote as 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The CMLE is clearly an M-estimator, since a maximization problem is easily
 turned into a minimization problem.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}\equiv(x_{i},y_{i})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $q(w_{i},\theta)\equiv-\log f(y_{i}|x_{i};\theta)$
\end_inset

.
 So, we can apply the results we obtained for M-estimators.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In a traditional framework, we would treat the 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 as constants appearing in the distribuion of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

, and we would define 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

 as the solution to
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \max_{\theta\in\Theta}}{\displaystyle \prod_{i=1}^{N}}f(y_{i}|x_{i};\theta)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Under independence, the product in expression (14) is the model for the
 joint density of 
\begin_inset Formula $(y_{1},\ldots,y_{N})$
\end_inset

, evaluated at the data.
 Because maximizing the function in (14) is the same as maximizing its natural
 log, we are led to problem (13).
 However, the arguments explaining why solving (14) should to a good estimator
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 are necessarily heuristic.
 By contrast, the analogy principle applies directly to problem (13), and
 we need not assume that the 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 are fixed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*

\emph on
(continued):
\emph default
 In the probit example, the log likelihood for observation 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $l_{i}(\theta)=y_{i}\log\Phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta)+(1-y_{i})\log\left[1-\Phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta)\right]$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
13.4 Consistency of CMLE
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now state a formal consistency result for the CMLE, which is a special
 case of the M-estimator consistency result.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
13.1 (Consistency of CMLE): 
\series default
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ (x_{i},y_{i}):i=1,2,\ldots\right\} $
\end_inset

 be a random sample with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}\in\mathscr{X}\subset\mathbb{R}^{K}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}\in\mathscr{Y}\subset\mathbb{R}^{G}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta\subset\mathbb{R}^{P}$
\end_inset

 be the parameter set, and denote the parametric model of the conditional
 density as 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ f(\cdot|x,\theta):x\in\mathscr{X},\theta\in\Theta\right\} $
\end_inset

.
 Assume that 
\series bold
(a)
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\cdot|x;\theta)$
\end_inset

 is a true density with respect to the measure 
\begin_inset Formula $v(dy)$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

, so that condition (6) holds; 
\series bold
(b)
\series default
 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}\in\Theta$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{o}(\cdot|x)=f(\cdot|x;\theta_{o}),\ \forall\ x\in\mathscr{X}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 is the unique solution to the problem (12); 
\series bold
(c)
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

 is a compact set; 
\series bold
(d)
\series default
 for each 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\in\Theta$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $l(\cdot,\theta)$
\end_inset

 is a Borel measurable function on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathscr{Y}\times\mathscr{X}$
\end_inset

; 
\series bold
(e)
\series default
 for each 
\begin_inset Formula $(y,x)\in\mathscr{Y}\times\mathscr{X}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $l(y,x,\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is a continuous function on 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

; and 
\series bold
(f)
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $|l(w,\theta)|\leq b(w),\ \forall\ \theta\in\Theta$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $E[b(w)]<\infty$
\end_inset

 .
 Then there exists a solution to problem (13), the CMLE 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{p\lim}\ \hat{\theta}=\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For the probit example, the log likelihood is clearly continuous in 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

.
 We can verify the moment condition 
\begin_inset Formula $(f)$
\end_inset

 if we bound certain moments of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and make the parameter space compact.
 But our primary concern is that densities are correctly specified.
 For example, the density for 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 will be incorrect if the latent error 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

 is not independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and normally distributed, or if the latent variable model is not linear
 to begin with.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
13.5 Asymptotic Normality and Asymptotic Variance Estimation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
13.5.1 Asymptotic Normality
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To derive the limiting distribution of the MLE, we will have to assume certain
 regularity conditions (see Theorem 12.3); in particular, we assume that
 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 is in the interior of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $l_{i}(\theta)$
\end_inset

 is twice continuously differentiable on the interior of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The 
\series bold
score of the log likelihood
\series default
 for observation 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is simply
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
s_{i}(\theta)\equiv\frac{\partial l_{i}(\theta)}{\partial\theta}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
\frac{\partial l_{i}}{\partial\theta_{1}}(\theta)\\
\vdots\\
\frac{\partial l_{i}}{\partial\theta_{P}}(\theta)
\end{array}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

a 
\begin_inset Formula $P\times1$
\end_inset

 vector.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*

\emph on
(continued):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
For the probit case, 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

 and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial l_{i}(\theta)}{\partial\theta}=y_{i}\left[\frac{\phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta)x_{i}^{\prime}}{\Phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta)}\right]-(1-y_{i})\left[\frac{\phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta)x_{i}^{\prime}}{\left[1-\Phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta)\right]}\right]
\]

\end_inset

Transposing this equation, and using a little algebra, gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
s_{i}(\theta)=\frac{\phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta)x_{i}\left[y_{i}-\Phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta)\right]}{\Phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta)\left[1-\Phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta)\right]}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the vast majority of cases, the score of the log-likelihood function
 has an important zero conditional mean property:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E\left[s_{i}(\theta_{o})|x_{i}\right]=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

In other words, when we evaluate the 
\begin_inset Formula $P\times1$
\end_inset

 score at 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

, and take its expectation with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\cdot|x_{i};\theta_{o})$
\end_inset

, the expectation is zero.
 Under condition (17), 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[s_{i}(\theta_{o})\right]=0$
\end_inset

, which was a key condition in deriving the asymptotic normality of the
 M-estimator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To show condition (17) generally, let 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{\theta}\left[\cdot|x_{i}\right]$
\end_inset

 denote conditional expectation with respect to the density 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\cdot|x_{i};\theta)$
\end_inset

 for any 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\in\Theta$
\end_inset

.
 Then, by definition,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E_{\theta}\left[s_{i}(\theta)|x_{i}\right]={\displaystyle \int_{\mathscr{Y}}}s(y,x_{i},\theta)f(y|x_{i};\theta)v(dy)
\]

\end_inset

If integration and differentiation can be interchanged on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{int}(\Theta)$
\end_inset

, that is, if
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\nabla_{\theta}\left({\displaystyle \int_{\mathscr{Y}}}f(y|x_{i};\theta)v(dy)\right)={\displaystyle \int_{\mathscr{Y}}}\nabla_{\theta}f(y|x_{i};\theta)v(dy)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

for all 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}\in\mathscr{X}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\in\mbox{int}(\Theta)$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
0={\displaystyle \int_{\mathscr{Y}}}\nabla_{\theta}f(y|x_{i};\theta)v(dy)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

since 
\begin_inset Formula $\int_{\mathscr{Y}}\nabla_{\theta}f(y|x_{i};\theta)v(dy)$
\end_inset

 is unity for all 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

, and therefore the partial derivatives with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 must be identically zero.
 But the right-hand side of equation (19) can be written as 
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \int_{\mathscr{Y}}}\left[\nabla_{\theta}l(y,x_{i},\theta)\right]f(y|x_{i};\theta)v(dy)$
\end_inset

.
 Putting in 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 yields (17).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*

\emph on
(continued): 
\emph default
Define 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}\equiv y_{i}-\Phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta_{o})=y_{i}-E(y_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 Then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
s_{i}(\theta_{o})=\frac{\phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta_{o})x_{i}u_{i}}{\Phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta_{o})\left[1-\Phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta_{o})\right]}
\]

\end_inset

and, since 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}|x_{i})=0$
\end_inset

, it follows that 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[s_{i}(\theta_{o})|x_{i}\right]=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Assuming that 
\begin_inset Formula $l_{i}(\theta)$
\end_inset

 is twice continuously differentiable on the interior of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

, let the Hessian for observation 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 be the 
\begin_inset Formula $P\times P$
\end_inset

 matrix of second partial derivatives of 
\begin_inset Formula $l_{i}(\theta)$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
H_{i}(\theta)\equiv\nabla_{\theta}s_{i}(\theta)=\nabla_{\theta}^{2}l_{i}(\theta)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The Hessian is a symmetric matrix that generally depend on 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},y_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 Since MLE is a maximization problem, the expected value of 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{i}(\theta)$
\end_inset

 is negative definite.
 Thus, we define
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
A_{o}\equiv-E\left[H_{i}(\theta_{o})\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which is generally a positive definite matrix when 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 is identified.
 Under standard regularity conditions, the asymptotic normality of the CMLE
 follows from Theorem 12.3: 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}(\hat{\theta}-\theta_{o})\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{a}\\
\sim
\end{array}N(0,A_{o}^{-1}B_{o}A_{o}^{-1})$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{o}\equiv\mbox{Var}\left[s_{i}(\theta)\right]\equiv E\left[s_{i}(\theta_{o})s_{i}(\theta_{o})^{\prime}\right]$
\end_inset

.
 We now show that 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{o}=A_{o}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We must assume enough smoothness such that the following interchange of
 integral and derivative is valid:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\nabla_{\theta}\left({\displaystyle \int_{\mathscr{Y}}}s_{i}(\theta)f(y|x_{i};\theta)v(dy)\right)={\displaystyle \int_{\mathscr{Y}}}\nabla_{\theta}\left[s_{i}(\theta)f(y|x_{i};\theta)\right]v(dy)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Then, taking the derivative of the identity
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\int_{\mathscr{Y}}s_{i}(\theta)f(y|x_{i};\theta)v(dy)\equiv E_{\theta}\left[s_{i}(\theta)|x_{i}\right]=0,\ \theta\in\mbox{int}(\Theta)
\]

\end_inset

and using equation (22), gives, for all 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\in\mbox{int}(\Theta)$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
-E_{\theta}\left[H_{i}(\theta)|x_{i}\right]=\mbox{Var}_{\theta}\left[s_{i}(\theta)|x_{i}\right]
\]

\end_inset

where the indexing by 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 denotes expectation and variance when 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\cdot|x;\theta)$
\end_inset

 is the density of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 When evaluated at 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

, we get a very important equality:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
-E\left[H_{i}(\theta_{o})|x_{i}\right]=E\left[s_{i}(\theta_{o})s_{i}(\theta_{o})^{\prime}|x_{i}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the expectation and variance are with respect to the true conditional
 distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Equation (23) is called the 
\series bold
conditional information matrix equality (CIME)
\series default
.
 Taking the expectation of equation (23) (with respect to the distribution
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

) and using the law of iterated expectations gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
-E\left[H_{i}(\theta_{o})\right]=E\left[s_{i}(\theta_{o})s_{i}(\theta_{o})^{\prime}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

or 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{o}=B_{o}$
\end_inset

.
 This relationship is best thought of as the 
\series bold
unconditional information matrix equality (UIME)
\series default
.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
13.2 (Asymptotic Normality of CMLE):
\series default
 
\emph on
Let the conditions of Theorem 13.1 hold.
 In addition, assume that 
\series bold
(a)
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}\in\mbox{int}(\Theta)$
\end_inset

; 
\series bold
(b)
\series default
 for each 
\begin_inset Formula $(y,x)\in\mathscr{Y}\times\mathscr{X}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $l(y,x,\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is twice continuously differentiable on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{int}(\Theta)$
\end_inset

; 
\series bold
(c)
\series default
 the interchanges of derivative and integral in equations (18) and (22)
 hold for all 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\in\mbox{int}(\Theta)$
\end_inset

; 
\series bold
(d)
\series default
 the elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla_{\theta}^{2}l(y,x,\theta)$
\end_inset

 are bounded in absolute value by a function 
\begin_inset Formula $b(y,x)$
\end_inset

 with finite expectation; and 
\series bold
(e)
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{o}$
\end_inset

 defined by expression (21) is positive definite.
 Then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sqrt{N}(\hat{\theta}-\theta_{o})\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{d}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}N(0,A_{o}^{-1})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and therefore
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\theta})=\frac{A_{o}^{-1}}{N}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
13.5.2 Estimating the Asymptotic Variance
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Estimating 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\theta})$
\end_inset

 requires estimating 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{o}$
\end_inset

.
 From the equalities derived previously, there are at least two possible
 estimators of 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{o}$
\end_inset

 in the CMLE context.
 In fact, under slight extensions of the regularity conditions in Theorem
 13.2, both of the matrices
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}-H_{i}(\hat{\theta}),\ \mbox{and}\ N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s_{i}(\hat{\theta})s_{i}(\hat{\theta})^{\prime}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

converge to 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{o}=B_{o}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\theta})}$
\end_inset

 can be taken to be either of the two matrices
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left[N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}-H_{i}(\hat{\theta})\right]^{-1},\ \mbox{or}\ \left[N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s_{i}(\hat{\theta})s_{i}(\hat{\theta})^{\prime}\right]^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and the asymptotic standard errors are the square roots of the diagonal
 elements of any of the matrices.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The first estimator, based on the Hessian of the log-likelihood, requires
 computing second derivatives.
 When the inverse exists, the estimate is positive definite because 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

 maximizies the objective funciton.
 The second estimator in equation (28), based on the outer product of the
 score, depends only on first derivatives of the log-likelihood funciton.
 Its primary drawback is that it can be poorly behaved in even moderate
 sample sizes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*

\emph on
(continued):
\emph default
 The Hessian for the probit log-likelihood is a mess.
 Fortunately, 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[H_{i}(\theta_{o})|x_{i}\right]$
\end_inset

 has a fairly simple form.
 Taking the derivative of equation (16) and using the product rule gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
H_{i}(\theta)=-\frac{\left\{ \phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta)\right\} ^{2}x_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}}{\Phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta)\left[1-\Phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta)\right]}+\left[y_{i}-\Phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta)\right]L(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta)
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $L(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta)$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times K$
\end_inset

 complicated function of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime}\theta$
\end_inset

 that we need not find explicitly.
 Now, when we evaluate this expression at 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 and note that 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left\{ \left[y_{i}-\Phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta_{o})\right]L(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta_{o})|x_{i}\right\} =\left[E(y_{i}|x_{i})-\Phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta_{o})\right]L(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta_{o})=0$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
-E\left[H_{i}(\theta_{o})|x_{i}\right]=A_{i}(\theta_{o})=\frac{\left\{ \phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta_{o})\right\} ^{2}x_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}}{\Phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta_{o})\left[1-\Phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\theta_{o})\right]}
\]

\end_inset

Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\theta})}$
\end_inset

 in probit analysis is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\frac{\left\{ \phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\theta})\right\} ^{2}x_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}}{\Phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\theta})\left[1-\Phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\theta})\right]}\right)^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which is always postitive definite when the inverse exists.
 Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times K$
\end_inset

 matrix for each 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
13.6 Hypothesis Testing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Given the asymptotic standard errors, it is easy to form asymptotic 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 statistics for testing single hypotheses.
 These 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 statistics are asymptotically distributed as standard normal.
 The three tests covered in Champter 12 are immediately appliable to the
 MLE case.
 The Wald statistic is the same as before.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Define the log-likelihood function for the entire sample by 
\begin_inset Formula $L(\theta)\equiv\sum_{i=1}^{N}l_{i}(\theta)$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

 be the unrestricted estimator, and let 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\theta}$
\end_inset

 be the estimator with the 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset

 nonredundant constraints imposed.
 Then, under the regularity conditions discussed before, the 
\series bold
likelihood ratio (LR) statistic
\series default
,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
LR\equiv2\left[L(\hat{\theta})-L(\tilde{\theta})\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

is distributed asymptotically as 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi_{Q}^{2}$
\end_inset

 under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 As with the Wald statistic, we cannot use LR as approximately 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi_{Q}^{2}$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 is on the boundary of the parameter set.
 The LR statistic is invariant to reparameterizing the conditional density.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The score or LM test is based on the restricted estimation only.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}(\tilde{\theta})$
\end_inset

 be the 
\begin_inset Formula $P\times1$
\end_inset

 score 
\begin_inset Formula $l_{i}(\theta)$
\end_inset

 evaluated at the restricted estimates 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\theta}$
\end_inset

.
 Then,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\tilde{s}_{i}\right)^{\prime}\left(-{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\tilde{H}_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\tilde{s}_{i}\right),\ \mbox{and}\ \left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\tilde{s}_{i}\right)^{\prime}\left(-{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\tilde{s}_{i}\tilde{s}_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\tilde{s}_{i}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

have limiting 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi_{Q}^{2}$
\end_inset

 distributions under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

.The first statistic is not guaranteed to be nonnegative and is not invariant
 to reparameterizations, but the outer product statistic is.
\end_layout

